## 7. Calculation
Domain-level: S̄_{b,d} = (1/N_d) Σ M_{p,b,d}, reported as B̄_d = (DFE̅_d, LKA̅_d, POI̅_d).

| Domain | DFE | LKA | POI | Status |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
| Online commenting | — | — | — | Requires respondent data |
| Sedentary behaviour | — | — | — | Requires respondent data |
| PET recycling | — | — | — | Requires respondent data |

## 8. Barrier-profile interpretation

The profile can indicate: which barrier has the highest reported intensity; the relative manifestation of the three barriers; whether profiles differ across target behaviours; which barriers merit further investigation; which intervention targets may be relevant.

Working threshold: subscale mean ≥ 3.0. The scale's midpoint (3 = "neither agree nor disagree") is the neutral anchor. Because every item is worded in the barrier-intensity direction, a mean at or above 3 means respondents are, on balance, agreeing that the barrier statements describe their experience rather than rejecting them — the barrier is net-present, not net-absent. That's the actual basis for the cutoff: it's where the scale itself switches from disagreement to agreement, not an arbitrary number picked for convenience. It stays a working threshold rather than a final one because it hasn't been checked against real score distributions yet — the domain-distribution survey will show whether 3.0 actually separates elevated from non-elevated barriers in practice, or whether real data cluster in a way that calls for a different cutoff.

The profile cannot independently establish: a confirmed barrier configuration; causality; interaction between barriers; that the two highest barriers necessarily constitute a pair; that small score differences above the threshold are meaningful.

Until the distribution-based validation is complete, report descriptively. Use: "DFE and LKA exceeded the working threshold and were descriptively more prominent than POI within this sample." Do not write: "DFE × LKA was conclusively identified as the active configuration."

## 9. How the 39-study corpus was used
Pathway: reported finding → barrier → facet → candidate item. The corpus provides the theoretical and empirical basis for the item pool — **it does not answer the items or generate subscale scores.**

### DFE item-development pathway
| Recurring finding | Facet | Item |
|---|---|---|
| Consequences appear distant | Consequence distance | DFE1 |
| Effects not immediately visible | Immediate visibility | DFE2 |
| One individual action seems insignificant | Individual impact | DFE3 |
| Difficult to connect action to outcomes | Action–outcome connection | DFE4 |
| Consequences not salient during action | Point-of-execution salience | DFE5 |

### LKA item-development pathway
| Recurring finding | Facet | Item |
|---|---|---|
| People don't know how to act | Procedural knowledge | LKA1 |
| Required actions unclear | Requirement awareness | LKA2 |
| People don't recognise when action is required | Situational recognition | LKA3 |
| Consequence information missing | Consequence knowledge | LKA4 |
| Instructions/rules confusing | Guidance comprehension | LKA5 |

### POI item-development pathway
| Recurring finding | Facet | Item |
|---|---|---|
| Behaviour requires excessive effort | Perceived effort | POI1 |
| Behaviour disrupts routines | Routine disruption | POI2 |
| Behaviour takes too much time | Perceived time cost | POI3 |
| Required actions create hassle | Procedural burden | POI4 |
| Difficult at point of execution | Situational inconvenience | POI5 |

## 10. Relationship to the literature evidence mapping
R_{b,d} = n_{b,d}/N_d × 100, N_d=13 per domain (see barrier_severity_rubric.md).

| Literature evidence mapping | I-BB Barrier Rubric |
|---|---|
| Unit: publication | Unit: respondent |
| Measures frequency in selected literature | Measures perceived barrier intensity |
| Depends on number/focus of studies | Can be administered for a new, unstudied behaviour |
| Produces a literature percentage | Produces DFE, LKA, POI scores |
| Supports construct development | Supports future behavioural diagnosis |

